# ruff: noqa: E501
# Authored prose records intentionally keep one paragraph per line.
"""Concise, section-targeted handbook material; no aggregate answer summaries."""

# Each line is an independently addressable section: heading | source | guidance.
PAYMENTS = """
Request acceptance|PROD-001|Record what the checkout response actually establishes. Acceptance means the request entered processing; completion requires a business outcome. Preserve the support reference when an acknowledgement is interrupted so later investigation can distinguish a pending instruction from a fresh submission.
Outbound connection boundary|ARCH-007|Separate waiting work from active PSP sessions. An increase in acquisition waits can occur while inbound workers remain healthy. Capture the effective connection setting with the observation rather than inferring it from a queue graph or a remembered baseline.
Retry amplification|ARCHREV-004|Compare attempts per original request with active connections and waiting work. Repeated attempts can intensify the same pressure that produced the original timeout. Increasing a resource limit without examining this feedback path can move the bottleneck to another dependency.
Admission behavior|ADR-021|Admission control decides whether additional work can enter the constrained path. Inspect what happens to deferred or rejected requests as well as accepted traffic. A smaller internal queue is not necessarily recovery if work disappeared without an attributable customer outcome.
Back pressure signals|RB-003-v2|Follow pressure signals from the outbound boundary to the caller. Confirm that upstream work observes the signal instead of immediately retrying. Retain request identifiers across deferral so a bounded queue does not become a source of ambiguous payment outcomes.
Circuit state|ADR-021|A circuit protects an unhealthy dependency by suppressing calls under the accepted design. Investigate why it opened and what evidence permits another attempt. Treat circuit behavior and provider recovery as separate observations; an open circuit is not itself the provider's root cause.
Retry budgets|PROD-007|Check whether repeated attempts consume a bounded allowance associated with the operation. Worker movement must not silently reset the budget. Preserve the original key and outcome reference so a client cannot create apparently fresh work merely by changing transport details.
Temporary capacity mitigation|CHG-139|A tuning release can improve waiting behavior without removing the underlying feedback mechanism. Preserve its actual configuration scope when comparing later events. Do not describe every temporary adjustment as the subsequent architectural redesign or assume that a completed change closed all recurrence actions.
Material change review|POL-007-v2|Compute the proposed increase against the documented baseline and consult the effective approval rule. Record downstream assumptions and rollback conditions. The existence of spare local resources does not establish that the external provider can safely absorb more simultaneous requests.
Emergency evidence packet|RB-010|Keep the incident qualification, requested action, authority, implementation time, and recovery evidence distinguishable. A clear packet allows reviewers to assess urgency and compliance independently. Contacting the responsible person is only a communication step; retain the actual approval decision separately.
Merchant operation identity|PROD-006-v2|Associate a retry with the merchant's business operation rather than the process that happened to send it. Preserve the key through interrupted acknowledgements. A replacement worker must find the persisted outcome instead of treating its first local observation as a new payment.
Conflicting key reuse|ADR-024|Compare the normalized payload when a key already exists. A different amount or destination must not inherit a previous successful result or silently start a new debit. Retain the explicit conflict outcome so support can explain why the request was rejected.
Replay-safe settlement|ADR-024|Trace uniqueness through settlement submission, not only the merchant-facing endpoint. An interface-level deduplication check is incomplete if another worker can independently submit the same business operation downstream. Retain the settlement reference beside the operation result for reconciliation.
Ambiguous acknowledgements|RB-004|An interrupted acknowledgement leaves an uncertainty to resolve. Check persisted payment and settlement outcomes before replay. Generating a new key to escape the uncertainty can create a second business instruction and should not be described as ordinary transport recovery.
Duplicate investigation units|ARCHREV-006|Maintain separate counts for transport attempts, commercial instructions, and executed debits. A failed-attempt metric can include rejected work and repeated attempts. It therefore cannot be relabeled as a count of duplicate debits without examining the business-level evidence.
Historical interface versions|PROD-006-v1|When investigating an older client, identify the contract effective for that client and event date. Do not silently apply a later mandatory-key promise to an earlier interface. Versioned specifications explain what behavior was expected at each point in the history.
Release correlation|CHG-118|A deployment timestamp is a useful investigation boundary, but temporal proximity is not sufficient causal evidence. Compare the changed path with the incident's observed mechanism. Preserve release chronology even when a later review supplies a more complete explanation.
Post-fix observation|ARCHREV-014|Retain later retry events with their individual mechanisms. A family label is useful for discovery but too broad to establish recurrence of one defect. Compare operation identity, persisted outcomes, and affected boundaries before deciding that a fix failed.
Event production and consumption|ARCH-002|Payment acceptance and settlement consumption cross an asynchronous boundary. Track the producer reference and consumer position independently. A successful synchronous response can coexist with downstream delay without either observation being false.
Backlog age|RB-008-v2|Measure the age of unprocessed events as well as the number waiting. A queue can shrink because work moved, expired, or was discarded. Recovery evidence should show processing and acknowledgement, not only a lower count in a dashboard.
Consumer partition progress|RB-005|Inspect the delayed subscription or partition rather than relying only on process availability. A running consumer group may conceal one stalled path. Record the last committed position with the observation time so another responder can verify actual progress.
Controlled catch-up|ADR-025|A recovery burst can overwhelm the next service even if the event consumer has spare capacity. Pace catch-up against downstream processing limits and preserve ordering. Reconciliation must confirm that accelerated consumption did not create a new ambiguity.
Reconciliation cutoff|POL-012|A reconciliation result describes work completed through a specified cutoff. Retain outstanding references beyond that boundary and explain their disposition separately. Do not advance the cutoff solely because an upstream producer has resumed normal operation.
Corporate report freshness|PROD-009|Explain the difference between report publication and the latest payment included. A newly opened report can still reflect an earlier processing cutoff. Customer support should preserve the report reference and cutoff when investigating missing recent activity.
Ledger race separation|RISK-006|Use the existence of a ledger-control reference as a reason to seek authorized evidence, not to infer its contents. Event backlog and posting order are different investigative paths. An operations handbook must not reproduce confidential accounting-adjustment evidence for an unauthorized reader.
Database pool diagnostics|ARCH-006|A database acquisition wait and an outbound HTTP pool wait can share a symptom while requiring different checks. Identify the resource and owning service before applying a runbook. Preserve original error codes alongside any plain-language summary.
Provider resets|ARCH-006|A reset identifies an interrupted connection boundary. It does not by itself show that local sessions were exhausted or that the provider's internal cause is known. Retain the connection phase and request outcome uncertainty during classification.
DNS before connection|ARCH-006|Resolution occurs before a new outbound session can be established. Confirm whether the endpoint was resolved before attributing failure to pool capacity. Existing healthy sessions may coexist with failures to create new ones when resolution is impaired.
Trust failures|ARCH-006|Certificate validation failure is different from an unavailable connection slot. Record the trust error and connection stage without disabling validation as an assumed remedy. The incident's approved recovery evidence, rather than the shared word timeout, determines classification.
Service-specific attribution|ARCH-002|Assign the incident to the service whose interruption is recorded. A downstream dependency named in its explanation does not automatically create a second incident. Preserve cross-service consequences without duplicating the same event in a collection analysis.
Incident identity|ARCH-006|Use the incident identifier to reconcile a report, change reference, and compendium entry. Multiple documents can describe one event. Keep separate source citations while preserving one event identity for any later calculation over incident records.
Interruption duration|POL-012|Read the start and end of the attributable interruption rather than substituting ticket-open time. The reporting purpose determines whether recovery observation or administrative closure matters. An unresolved financial item can remain after an operational interruption ends.
Unknown customer impact|PROD-005|A failed attempt does not identify a unique customer. Repeated attempts and shared corporate operators can make those units diverge substantially. Preserve unknown customer counts explicitly and avoid translating attempt totals into customer totals without additional evidence.
Financial-loss limits|PROD-009|Delayed reporting and failed attempts can create concern without establishing a measured loss. A monetary assessment needs its own recorded amounts and methodology. Do not introduce an assumed average payment value to make an incomplete operational metric appear financially precise.
Observation windows|ARCHREV-013|State the periods being compared and retain qualifying residual incidents. Unequal exposure windows and missing traffic denominators limit the interpretation of a before-and-after comparison. The reports support observation; they do not independently supply a controlled causal experiment.
Source effective dates|POL-007-v2|Publication, effective date, and event date answer different questions. A policy may be published before it takes effect, while a report may be written after the event. Select the rule applicable to the action rather than simply the newest file.
Current status boundary|RB-009|Historical documents explain completed events and procedures. A present incident enquiry needs a current operational record with an update time. If that record is unavailable, preserve uncertainty instead of substituting a recent release note as a status report.
Escalation identity|RB-013|Resolve whether the request concerns an owner, incident commander, manager, or shift responder. Those roles can be held by different people. Use the appropriate dated or current authority before requesting contact details, and retain the service identifier through the lookup.
Customer-visible uncertainty|PROD-001|Support should distinguish pending confirmation from confirmed failure or completion. A transport error alone may leave the business result unresolved. Preserve the original support reference so the customer is not encouraged to submit duplicate instructions to discover the outcome.
Safe incident handover|RB-003-v2|Transfer observed symptoms, attempted actions, preserved references, and unresolved hypotheses together. The next responder should know which boundary was checked and which remains uncertain. A handover that only says fixed or still broken loses the evidence needed to continue investigation.
"""

RISK = """
Decision scope|POL-019|Begin a control review by naming the decision being assessed and its effective period. A policy recommendation, approved rule, and implemented setting are different records. Preserve their sequence so a reviewer can see whether the required authority existed when the action occurred.
Corporate segment evidence|RISK-003|A segment-based allowance depends on verified eligibility. Retain the evidence establishing that status and distinguish it from a customer's desire for a higher limit. Product demand can motivate a review without supplying the eligibility evidence needed for a particular customer.
Shared daily usage|ARCH-003|Examine whether usage is shared across operators and channels for the corporate customer. Testing only a single user can miss a bypass at the account boundary. Preserve the accounting interval and policy version alongside the resulting limit decision.
Committee conditions|MTG-003|Read conditional approval together with its outstanding requirements. A committee's support for a proposal does not mean every prerequisite has been satisfied. Track the policy and implementation records that convert the decision into an operative customer rule.
Policy supersession|POL-002-v2|Retain the prior version and the replacement's effective boundary. Historical decisions need the version in force at the time. A present search result should not erase evidence that a different allowance legitimately applied earlier.
Limit exceptions|RB-006|Distinguish record correction from a request to depart from policy. Correcting a mistaken customer segment does not grant unrestricted discretion to increase an allowance. Any exception requires attributable authority and a scope that can be examined later.
Channel consistency|PROD-012|Compare web and API treatment of the same corporate customer and accounting interval. A channel change must not reset consumption or verification state. Differences in wording should not create contradictory explanations of the governing rule.
Review cadence|POL-019|A scheduled review is an opportunity to assess whether the rule remains appropriate. It is not an automatic permission to change it. Retain the evidence considered, recommendation, authorized disposition, and effective replacement where one is approved.
Model baseline|RISK-001|Preserve the model release and comparable decision sample before evaluating an adjustment. Changes in cohort composition can affect observed complaints independently of model behavior. Review conclusions should identify the comparison population and its limitations.
Feature confidentiality|RISK-002|Authorized feature investigation serves a different audience from customer support. Retain extraction time and decision context within the permitted evidence boundary. Do not make detailed feature descriptions available by copying them into a broadly accessible summary.
False-positive interpretation|RISK-001|A complaint is a signal for examination, not automatically a confirmed false positive. Link it to the reviewed disposition before drawing conclusions. Keep unknown outcomes visible so unresolved complaints do not become fabricated favorable results.
Manual-review capacity|MTG-005|A threshold adjustment can transfer work into manual review. Assess queue behavior and review capacity alongside customer friction. A decrease in automated rejection does not establish improvement if unresolved review work simply accumulates elsewhere.
Fraud exposure|POL-010-v2|Evaluate the possibility of additional fraud exposure even when customer friction falls. The available operational evidence may not quantify a monetary effect. Record that limitation instead of converting a directional tradeoff into an unsupported financial claim.
Bounded approval|MTG-005|Identify the exact adjustment and observation obligations accepted by the committee. Approval for one bounded change is not standing authority for further tuning. Preserve the rollback condition and responsible review owner with the release packet.
Model versus configuration|CHG-109|A model release and a threshold configuration are distinct changes. Retain both references when examining a decision. Without that separation, an investigator may attribute behavior to the wrong modification or overlook a cohort-mapping issue.
Rollback authority|RB-007|A rollback still changes a control and requires the applicable authority. Capture the trigger and pre-action evidence before implementation. Returning to an earlier setting does not remove the need to verify customer and review-queue behavior afterward.
Sanitized communication|PROD-011|Customer wording should explain the permitted next step and preserve a support reference. It should not reveal the rule features or imply that a customer can obtain approval by repeating a pattern. Pending review must remain distinguishable from final rejection.
Measured benefit|RISK-005|Define the unit behind any claimed benefit. Failed attempts, customers, transactions, and currency amounts are not interchangeable. If monetary loss was not measured, retain the unknown rather than introduce an average-value assumption to complete a business case.
Capacity materiality|POL-007-v2|Evaluate the requested increase against the effective capacity rule and documented baseline. Retain the calculation in the approval packet. A proposed doubling and a small adjustment can share an operational aim while falling into different governance paths.
Emergency qualification|RB-010|The expedited route depends on the qualifying incident and applicable policy. Keep the evidence establishing urgency separate from the proposed technical action. A responder's assertion that a change is urgent does not replace the recorded incident qualification.
Contemporaneous authority|POL-007-v2|Identify the approval decision that existed before implementation under the permitted path. A directory result helps find a responsible person but does not prove they approved the action. Preserve decision time and scope rather than only a name.
Retrospective clock|POL-011|Start the review interval from implementation, not ticket completion. Record the implementation and approval timestamps as independent fields. A late explanatory note can improve the record without changing whether the original deadline was met.
Restoration evidence|CHG-160|A change can restore service and still leave a governance obligation unmet. Preserve the observed technical result rather than changing it to match the compliance verdict. Reviewers need both timelines to explain the case accurately.
Missed approval deadline|RISK-007|Assess timeliness against the evidence available at the required boundary. A later approval cannot be represented as timely simply because the incident was resolved. Keep the exception visible and assign a corrective process action.
Emergency review actions|MTG-011|Follow-up actions should address why the deadline or evidence requirement was missed. Rewriting the original timestamps is not remediation. Retain the operational result, the control finding, and the proposed prevention mechanism as distinct parts of the record.
Finding definition|RISK-008|An audit finding must describe the gap and the evidence that made it observable. Avoid replacing a missing disposition with a presumption of approval. The remediation plan should address the identified gap rather than merely produce a new tool or screen.
Population completeness|RB-011|A review cannot establish coverage without the population assigned to the cycle. Retain that population with subsequent decisions and exceptions. Otherwise, a list of completed reviews may conceal access items that were never examined.
Monthly obligation|POL-013-v2|A scheduled monthly process needs evidence of actual completion for each cycle. Assignment and reminder records demonstrate workflow activity, not the final disposition. Unanswered items must remain distinguishable from reviewed and approved access.
Implementation evidence|CHG-167|A workflow release establishes that a mechanism was delivered. It does not establish that reviewers used it correctly in later cycles. Preserve release checks and operating evidence separately so assurance can follow the complete chain.
Exception disposition|RB-011|An exception must retain its reason, responsible owner, and current evidence of disposition. Closing the surrounding review does not automatically resolve every exception. Keep pending removals separate from access changes that have actually completed.
Independent verification|RISK-009|Verification follows implementation and examination of completed operating cycles. The verifier should be able to trace the population, decisions, and resulting changes. Independence matters because an implementation owner's completion statement alone cannot establish sustained operation.
Closure scope|MTG-012|Closure applies to the finding and verification period actually examined. It does not certify every privileged system indefinitely. Retain the original scope and ongoing obligations so later readers do not interpret closure as a permanent waiver.
Restricted implementation|POL-018|Sensitive control configuration has a narrower audience than general review obligations. A review summary should identify the applicable evidence without reproducing privileged implementation details. Source access boundaries remain relevant when information is combined into a manual.
Reconciliation evidence|POL-012|Keep accepted instructions, consumed events, and reconciled entries distinguishable. An unmatched item requires a supported disposition. A report that is delayed or incomplete should state its cutoff instead of implying that all current activity has been reconciled.
Accounting adjustments|RISK-006|A manual adjustment requires its own rationale and review evidence. A balanced result after correction does not establish that the original control operated properly. Link the accounting outcome to the incident without merging unrelated event-backlog and posting-order mechanisms.
Report completeness|RISK-005|Assess completeness relative to the reported cutoff and outstanding work. Publication time alone is insufficient. Customer communication should explain what is represented and what remains unresolved without inventing a financial loss from delayed visibility.
DR acceptance|POL-014|Define recovery in terms of the required customer journey and its dependencies. Successful infrastructure checks remain useful evidence even when a downstream checkpoint fails. Record partial recovery explicitly instead of averaging different outcomes into an overall success label.
Notification verification|RB-012-v2|A controlled notification should traverse the same relevant boundaries as the exercised journey. Retain delivery and acknowledgement outcomes separately from database recovery. An untested notification path cannot inherit another component's successful result.
Ownership during transition|POL-015|Use effective dates to assess accountability during a handover. Preserve original design attribution while tracking transferred operational duties. A later catalog entry cannot rewrite who was accountable under an earlier operating model.
Evidence limitations|RISK-009|A review conclusion should state what was examined and what remains outside scope. Unknown measurements and missing records are limits on the answer, not opportunities to complete it from intuition. Preserve those limits when summarizing for a different audience.
"""

PLATFORM = """
Journey boundaries|ARCH-002|Map the customer journey across synchronous requests and asynchronous work. Each boundary has its own acceptance and completion evidence. A successful response from one component does not establish that every downstream consumer or reporting stage has completed.
Design attribution|ARCH-001|Retain the team named in the original dated design when discussing authorship. Operational responsibility can change later without making that historical attribution wrong. Do not replace it with a present catalog result unless the question explicitly concerns current ownership.
Operating-model transfer|ADR-027|A handover transfers defined responsibilities rather than retroactively changing the design record. Check runbooks, unresolved actions, escalation duties, and catalog stewardship. These obligations may move together but still require distinct acceptance evidence.
Effective handover date|POL-015|Identify when the transfer takes effect rather than using the publication date of the minutes as a substitute. Questions about the transition boundary need the effective policy. Original design and later operation may therefore have different correct owners.
Catalog migration record|CHG-172|A dated migration explains why a catalog changed at that time. It does not provide a live roster indefinitely. Retain the source decision and migration reference so the historical transition can be reconstructed independently of later catalog edits.
Escalation versus authorship|RB-013|The designer of a component is not automatically its present responder. Resolve the requested responsibility before identifying a person. A historical architecture citation can answer who designed a service while remaining unsuitable for today's escalation contact.
Shift boundaries|RB-009|An on-call assignment applies to a time interval. Preserve the requested time and service identity when seeking the responder. A name copied from an earlier incident cannot establish coverage for a later shift or an arbitrary historical date.
Incident command|RB-009|Incident coordination and technical shift coverage are different roles. Follow the incident record for command responsibility and the applicable roster for the responder. Do not collapse them merely because one person happened to perform both roles in an earlier event.
Dependency inventories|ADR-026|An inventory must follow customer-visible work beyond the primary database. Trace event delivery, consumption, notification preparation, and acknowledgement where they are required. An omitted dependency can make infrastructure restoration look more complete than the customer experience.
Database recovery scope|RB-012-v1|Database restoration demonstrates the checks that were actually performed. It does not establish that an omitted application dependency recovered. Preserve the old checklist when examining why an earlier exercise produced an incomplete result.
Notification recovery path|ARCHREV-012|A notification journey has its own checkpoints after data is restored. Identify the path from event availability to acknowledged delivery. The ability to read restored records is a prerequisite observation, not proof that customers received the intended messages.
Revised exercise procedure|RB-012-v2|Use the revised checklist for exercises after its effective boundary and retain the previous version for historical analysis. Each exercise still needs execution evidence. Publication of a better procedure does not retroactively make an earlier test pass.
Partial-recovery reporting|PROD-010|Communicate which function was verified and which remains impaired. A precise partial result is more useful than a broad success label when the journey crosses several dependencies. Preserve the recovery reference so support can connect customer observations with the tested path.
Controlled notification checks|CHG-132|Keep exercise messages distinguishable from real customer traffic. Record the acknowledgement and test reference so the result can be verified without misleading customers. Adding a checkpoint is implementation work; passing a later exercise is operating evidence.
Historical regional evidence|POL-014|An exercise result describes its date and scenario. It is not continuous health telemetry. When asked about current regional conditions, identify the need for present operational evidence rather than extrapolating from a successful historical database restoration.
Request correlation|PROD-002|Correlation references connect observations across a request journey. They are useful for investigation but do not necessarily define uniqueness of a business operation. Check the effective interface contract before assigning a stronger guarantee to an identifier.
Operation persistence|ADR-024|The durable operation result must survive movement between workers. A process restart should not make a known operation appear new. Trace the persisted result through settlement so local request handling cannot conceal a downstream replay boundary.
Client compatibility|PROD-006-v1|Older clients may have operated under a different retry contract. Preserve that version when reconstructing their behavior. A later interface requirement is evidence of a changed expectation, not proof that every previous client violated the newer rule.
Payload consistency|PROD-006-v2|A repeated key must refer to the same intended request. Compare the normalized payload and retain an explicit conflict when it differs. Silently accepting altered content under an old key makes investigation and customer explanations unreliable.
Worker concurrency|ARCH-007|Worker availability and outbound connection availability are separate resources. Inspect both before changing concurrency. More workers can increase waiting pressure when the constrained boundary is downstream and cannot accept additional simultaneous work.
Dependency pressure|ARCHREV-004|Pressure can propagate through retries as well as direct traffic. Follow repeated attempts to their original request so the apparent arrival rate is not mistaken for entirely new customer demand. This distinction matters when evaluating capacity and admission behavior.
Bounded admission design|ADR-021|A bounded path needs defined behavior for work that cannot enter. Preserve customer outcome references for rejected or deferred operations. Resource protection alone is insufficient if the application loses the information needed to explain what happened to the request.
Timeout interpretation|ARCH-006|Identify the boundary whose deadline expired. A database wait, DNS lookup, provider response, and client acknowledgement can all surface as timeout language. The original error code and service identifier help select the relevant investigation path.
Provider session lifecycle|ARCH-007|A session can remain occupied after a caller stops waiting. Compare local timeout behavior with downstream completion and cleanup. Otherwise, client-visible failure may leave hidden work retaining the resource that the next request needs.
Circuit recovery checks|RB-003-v2|Inspect circuit behavior alongside dependency observations and retry-budget use. Reopening a path needs evidence that the dependency can accept the work. A circuit transition is a control action, not independent confirmation of a permanent root-cause fix.
Event position tracking|RB-005|Record committed position with observation time and subscription identity. A position without that context is difficult to compare across handovers. Investigate the delayed path explicitly instead of inferring its progress from overall process uptime.
Backlog release rate|RB-008-v2|Draining a backlog changes the load delivered downstream. Preserve ordering and monitor acknowledgements while increasing processing. A faster consumer can move the bottleneck to reconciliation rather than resolve the customer's delayed result.
Settlement completion evidence|POL-012|Follow accepted instructions into reconciled entries before declaring downstream completion. Outstanding references remain meaningful even after the producer resumes. A final report must represent the cutoff it actually reconciled, not merely the latest event received.
Report publication boundary|PROD-005|Publication and content freshness are separate attributes. Preserve the report reference and completed cutoff when comparing what a customer saw. Reopening or republishing a report does not independently demonstrate that newer payment activity was incorporated.
Versioned configuration|CHG-139|Record the actual scope of a configuration adjustment. A wait-handling change and a connection-ceiling increase are different modifications even when both are called tuning. Later analysis needs the precise boundary changed, not a generic release label.
Rollback evidence|CHG-144|Rollback readiness and observed release success answer different questions. Retain the rollback assessment without treating it as proof that rollback was used. A historical implementation record must preserve what occurred, what was tested, and what remained under observation.
Deployment versus present state|CHG-153|A release record establishes an action at its implementation date. The service may have changed afterward. Present-version questions therefore require the deployment authority rather than an assumption that the most relevant historical fix is still the active release.
Diagnostic logging scope|CHG-178|Logging changes can affect investigation without changing customer-facing semantics. Compare the released path with an incident before assigning causation. A recent deployment is relevant chronology, but the mechanism needs independent supporting observations.
Service identity in handovers|RB-009|Keep the selected service identifier through incident review, dependency lookup, and escalation. Similar names or shared symptoms can otherwise shift the investigation to another component. Explicit identity is especially useful when a conversation moves from general payments to one incident.
Policy lookup by date|POL-007-v1|Select the rule effective when the action occurred. An older qualitative standard and a later numerical threshold can lead to different historical compliance questions. Preserve both versions so current retrieval does not erase the original decision context.
Proposal and implementation|MTG-017|A meeting can propose a remedy without authorizing it. Follow the later decision and release records before saying a proposal became the implemented design. Keeping these stages separate prevents an abandoned option from appearing to be the current architecture.
Residual incidents|ARCHREV-013|Retain qualifying events after a rollout even if they are smaller than earlier failures. Their presence constrains claims of elimination. Comparing periods still requires explicit windows and exposure limitations rather than relying on a visually simpler narrative.
Interface acceptance checks|PROD-001|Test uncertain outcomes as well as straightforward completion. Customer-facing state must remain consistent with the business evidence when a transport acknowledgement is lost. Support references should survive that path so investigation can proceed without fresh duplicate submissions.
Cross-document references|ADR-027|A reference identifies another record and its purpose; it does not make the referring document authoritative for every fact in the target. Follow the relevant version and section when the question depends on a particular decision, procedure, or implementation action.
Historical reference discipline|RB-013|Keep publication date, effective interval, and responsibility type visible in a technical answer. The newest document is not always the right source for a historical question. Explicit time and scope let apparently different records coexist without creating a false contradiction.
"""


def sections(text):
    result = [line.split("|", 2) for line in text.strip().splitlines()]
    assert len(result) == 40, len(result)
    return result
